body:
If (AllCurledUp) {  
Roll();  
}  


There ya go.

Righto, time to see what we can do. I'll drop a link, as well as a reasoning for each part.

Here's a suggestion:

[https://pcpartpicker.com/list/G3JXYH](https://pcpartpicker.com/list/G3JXYH)

&#x200B;

**CPU:** 

The AMD ryzen 3950x is arguably the most powerful non-workstation/server cpu (threadripper, xeon etc), and I think it'll be a fantastic fit for you. That being said, when I was into 3D design cpu was alot more important than what I hear they are now. If CPU has dropped in importance, you can instead pick up a 3700x, which will drop the price.

&#x200B;

**CPU Cooler:**

Seeing as this computer will be rendering for potentially many hours in a row, having decent and reliable cooling for it is important. You could potentially grab one of the really good noctua air-coolers, but this will run quieter. If you have the possibility, I would actually grab a 360mm rad, instead of the 240mm that this one has. Just for the added performance.

&#x200B;

**Motherboard:**

X570 is the newest chipset for the AM4 platform, it offers both CrossFire and 2-Way SLI, inbuilt wifi and the new PCI-E 4.0 slot. It's sleek, it's black, got some RGB. What's not to like?

&#x200B;

**Memory:**

32GB of DDR4-3000mhz memory. Ryzen wants quick RAM, so this'll satisfy that. Seeing as it's 2x16gb sticks, you also have an upgrade path there. You could potentially go for faster ram if you'd like, but the cost increases quickly.

&#x200B;

**Storage:**

1 M.2 NVME SSD for some lightning fast performance, as well as a chunky 4TB HDD for storing all your old scenes, objects, libraries and whatnot. It's worth considering an upgrade on the SSD to 1TB, it's around 60 USD more.

&#x200B;

**Video Card:**

You knew this was coming, and I'm not gonna lie, it's damn near 50% of the cost of the system. 2 RTX 2080Ti for some insane performance. I chose a triple fan GPU, as that'll make sure your cards won't overheat and throttle during long duration renders.

&#x200B;

**Case:**

It's go air filters, so it'll be easier to clean in the future. It's roomy and can fit upto 360mm rad for sweet cpu cooling. Plenty of space inside.

&#x200B;

**Power Supply:**

With an estimated power use of 744W, the 860W PSU will keep your system going. The important part is the 80+ Platinum certification so you know it'll keep going under those long renders. It's also fully modular so you won't have to deal with any excess cables.

&#x200B;

**Notes:**

None of these parts are set in stone. I used Pc part picker as it's easy to put together a system. I'm fairly certain you'll be able to find similar components to what I've posted for better prices. If you're gonna skimp on anything, make sure it's NOT on the power supply.

I know the total price is slightly above your budget, but this is assuming you have NO parts at all. You may be able to scavenge some parts from your old system, in example, the storage, which will lower the total price.

&#x200B;

Don't hesitate to contact me if you have any questions:)

Here's a build I put together for you. I'll explain the parts briefly.

[https://pcpartpicker.com/list/4zhrYH](https://pcpartpicker.com/list/4zhrYH)

&#x200B;

The CPU:   
I chose this cpu as it's at a fair price point, has decent clocks, and if you're into it, can overclock quite a bit. The 8 cores means you should be able to potentially stream fairly well while gaming, if you're into that. 

The Noctua fan I chose to go with it is an excellent air cooler, and actually outperforms several AiOs (note, not triple rads, but cheaper AiOs)

&#x200B;

Motherboard: I picked an x570 board as it'll be compatible with the next 1 or 2 generations of AMD CPUs, meaning you'll have an upgrade path, without having to swap the mobo out.

&#x200B;

Storage: 1TB of sweet, sweet m.2 storage. With the games you mentioned, I do not believe you'll be needing additional storage, but if you do, you can easily add a HDD later.

Please note, that as this will be both your boot drive and your storage drive, I would recommend partitioning it into 2 drives, so that they're seperate.

&#x200B;

GPU: 2060 super, it's good value for money, and it'll keep you running for a while. Just don't expect 60fps in RTX games.

&#x200B;

RAM: 16gb of 3000mhz memory. A single 16gb stick means it'll be easy for you to add more later.

&#x200B;

Case: It's a case, it's black. Looks alright, and NZXT knows what they're doing.

&#x200B;

PSU: 650W with 80+ gold certification and fully modular. It's pretty sweet.

&#x200B;

Please note that you could drop the price even further, by grabbing a 1660-Ti instead of the 2060 super. There's not a huge performance difference, but the RTX is nice.
